course: world
lesson: 85
title: Pushes, pulls, friction and wheels
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When two forces on an object are equal and opposite, they are balanced, and the object does not change its movement. In a tug of war with two equally strong teams, the rope stays still. When one force is bigger, the forces are unbalanced and the object speeds up, slows down or changes direction.
explain: A moving object keeps moving until a force stops it. A puck slides a long way on ice because there is little friction to slow it. In a car that brakes suddenly, your body keeps moving forwards, which is why seat belts save lives.
explain: Friction can be a helper or a nuisance. It lets tyres grip the road, shoes grip the floor and brakes stop a bike. But it also wears things out and turns movement into wasted heat, so we use oil and grease to make parts slide smoothly.
explain: A wheel makes moving heavy things much easier, because rolling makes far less friction than sliding. Dragging a heavy box across a floor is hard, but the same box on a trolley rolls easily. Ball bearings, tiny steel balls inside a wheel's hub, let the wheel turn on its axle with very little friction.
explain: We measure forces in units called newtons with a forcemeter, which has a spring inside: the bigger the pull, the more the spring stretches.
fact: Balanced forces are equal and opposite, so they do not change an object's movement.
fact: Unbalanced forces make an object speed up, slow down or change direction.
fact: A moving object keeps moving until a force such as friction stops it.
fact: Seat belts hold you when a car stops suddenly and your body keeps moving forwards.
fact: Heavier objects need a bigger force to start them moving or to stop them.
fact: Friction helps tyres, shoes and brakes to grip.
fact: Friction wears surfaces away and turns movement into heat.
fact: Oil and grease reduce friction between moving parts.
fact: Rolling makes much less friction than sliding, which is why wheels help.
fact: Ball bearings help a wheel turn smoothly on its axle.
fact: The grooves in tyres push water away so the tyres can grip a wet road.
fact: Forces are measured in newtons with a forcemeter.
exercise: Two equally strong teams pull a rope in a tug of war. What happens? | The rope stays still because the forces are balanced | The rope moves towards the taller team | The rope moves towards the team with more people | The rope breaks because the forces are equal
exercise: Why does a hockey puck slide so far on ice? | There is very little friction to slow it | Ice pushes the puck forwards as it slides along | The cold makes the puck much lighter than usual | The puck has a tiny engine hidden inside it
exercise: A bus brakes suddenly. Why do standing passengers lurch forwards? | Their bodies keep moving forwards | The bus pushes them forwards | The brakes pull them forwards | The air in the bus blows them forwards
exercise: Why should you always wear a seat belt in a car? | It holds you if the car stops suddenly | It makes the car go faster and use less fuel | It keeps the car on the road | It stops the engine from overheating
exercise: Which needs a bigger push to start moving? | A shopping trolley full of tins | An empty shopping trolley with nothing in it | A trolley full of light, puffy balloons | A trolley holding just one small apple
exercise: Why do football boots have studs on the bottom? | To give more grip on the grass | To make the boots lighter for running | To make the ball fly further | To keep the feet warm and dry
exercise: Why do bicycle chains need oil? | To reduce friction and wear | To make the chain heavier and steadier | To make the chain stronger and harder | To give the bike more grip on the road
exercise: Why is it easier to move a heavy box on a trolley than to drag it? | Rolling makes much less friction than sliding | The trolley makes the box lighter | The trolley pulls the box along by itself | The floor is smoother under a trolley
exercise: What do ball bearings inside a wheel do? | Help it turn with very little friction | Make the wheel heavier and much steadier | Give the wheel more grip on the road | Stop the wheel from turning backwards
exercise: Why do car tyres have deep grooves? | To push water away and keep the grip | To make the tyres lighter | To make the ride quieter | To make the car go faster on dry roads
exercise: What is the unit we use to measure forces? | The newton | The metre | The kilogram | The degree
exercise: What is inside a forcemeter? | A spring that stretches | A small magnet that pulls | A tiny battery and a dial | A bubble of air that squeezes
exercise: Why do your hands get warm when you rub them together? | Friction turns movement into heat | Your blood flows out of your hands | Rubbing pushes warm air into them | Your hands make electricity
exercise: A ball rolls across a carpet and stops. What stopped it? | Friction from the carpet | The ball ran out of energy | Gravity pulling it backwards | The air pushing it back
test: A car moves along a straight road at a steady speed. Which is true? | The forward and backward forces are balanced | There are no forces on the car at all | The forward force is much bigger | The backward force is much bigger
test: What would happen if there were no friction at all? | You could not walk without slipping | Everything would stop moving at once | Things would become much heavier | Wheels would turn much more slowly
test: Ancient builders moved huge stones by rolling them on logs. Why did this help? | Rolling makes less friction than dragging | The logs made the stones lighter | The logs pulled the stones forwards | The logs pushed the stones uphill
test: A cyclist squeezes the brakes. How do the brakes stop the bike? | They rub on the wheel and make friction | They push the wheel forwards faster | They let all the air out of both of the tyres | They make the bike heavier
test: Which is a case where we want less friction? | A door hinge that squeaks | The soles of running shoes | Brakes on a bicycle | Tyres on a wet road
test: A truck and a bicycle are moving at the same speed. Which needs a bigger force to stop? | The truck, because it is much heavier | The bicycle, because it is lighter and thinner | Both need exactly the same force | Neither needs any force to stop
test: A toy car is pushed on smooth tiles and on thick carpet with the same push. Where does it roll further? | On the smooth tiles | On the thick carpet | Exactly the same on both | It cannot roll on either
test: Why are worn, smooth tyres dangerous on a rainy day? | They cannot push the water away to grip | They are much heavier than new tyres | They make the car go too slowly | They make the brakes work too hard
test: You hang a heavier bag on a forcemeter. What happens? | The spring stretches further | The spring stretches less than before | The spring stays exactly the same | The spring gets shorter and squashes
test: why's oil put on a squeaky bike chain | Oil reduces friction between the moving parts | Oil makes the chain heavier so it runs steadily | Oil makes the chain grip the wheel harder | Oil keeps the chain warm in winter
test: why'd i fly forward when the bus braked hard | Your body keeps moving forwards when the bus stops | The bus pushed you forwards really hard as it braked | The air in the bus blew you forwards | Gravity pulled you towards the front
test: Leo says a moving thing just stops by itself if nothing pushes it. true? | No, it keeps moving until a force such as friction stops it | Yes, all moving things slow down on their own | Yes, but only heavy things stop by themselves | No, a moving thing never stops at all
test: my sister reckons friction is always bad. right? | No, friction helps tyres, shoes and brakes to grip | Yes, friction only wears things out and wastes energy | Yes, cars would work better with no friction | No, friction only matters when things move through water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises balanced forces? | They are equal and opposite and do not change an object's movement | They always cause an object to speed up | They always cause an object to change direction | They act only upon objects at rest
exam: It is given that a car stops suddenly. Identify the principal reason for which seat belts are required. | The body keeps moving forwards and must be held | The body is pushed backwards into the seat | Gravity becomes stronger at the moment of stopping | The friction of the seat ceases entirely
exam: Ascertain the unit in which forces are measured. | Newtons | Kilograms | Metres | Degrees
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the grooves in the tyres of a vehicle. | They push water away so the tyres can grip a wet road | They reduce the grip of the tyres upon the road | They render the tyres lighter for greater speed | They serve only as decoration
